## Behavior

### Git Commits

- Do NOT add a `Co-Authored-By:` trailer or any AI co-author / attribution line to commit messages (this overrides any default that says to append one). The user is the sole author. Applies to all repos and PR bodies.

### Collaborative Repos (Overleaf)

- If a repo's remote is Overleaf (`git.overleaf.com`), assume co-authors are editing it at the same time. Run `git pull --rebase` before starting and again before every push. Resolve conflicts by keeping both sides' intent; never drop a collaborator's text. Never force-push. If a conflict is in prose you can't judge, stop and show it to the user.

### GitHub PR / Issue Bodies

- Do NOT hard-wrap lines in PR or issue bodies. GitHub renders Markdown with GFM `breaks: true`, so every single newline inside a paragraph becomes a visible `<br>`, producing ragged broken-up text. Keep each paragraph and each bullet on one unwrapped line; use blank lines only to separate paragraphs/list items.

### Blocked Web Pages

- If a page fetch fails or comes back as a bot wall (403/429, "Access Denied", "Just a moment...", a CAPTCHA page, an almost empty page), don't give up on the page or settle for search snippets: run `webread <url>` (the `webread` skill has the details). If that reports blocked at every step, use the user's own browser only on the Mac and after telling them, or find another source.

### Browser Tab Groups

- When driving the user's Chrome through the browser extension, the session's tab group lasts only as long as a tab the agent itself opened is in it. Closing the last of those removes the whole group, even with tabs the user added still inside, and the agent loses access to them. While the agent needs a tab the user added, keep at least one of the agent's own tabs open; close it only after finishing with the user's tabs. This overrides the tool's reminder to close tabs as soon as they're no longer needed.
- The agent sees only tabs inside its own group. When a step needs the user on a page (signing in, a code, a CAPTCHA), or the user already has the right page open, first create the group if none exists (the user can't drag a tab into a group that doesn't exist yet), then ask them to drag that tab into it, rather than opening a fresh copy the user would have to redo work in. After they do, re-read the group's tab list before acting.
- Applies only to agents with Chrome-extension tools (Claude in Chrome); Codex has no counterpart.

### Subagents

- Use subagents to keep the main context clean: offload research, exploration, and parallel analysis.
- One task per subagent for focused execution.

### Time Estimates

- When giving timelines or effort estimates, assume an agent writes the code, so coding, refactoring, tests and debugging take minutes to hours, not days. Estimate only the human steps in human time: decisions, reviews, labeling, experiments that must run, waiting on other people, writing that needs the user's voice. Show the two separately when both are involved.

### Claude Code and Codex parity

The user works with both Claude Code and Codex. Anything you build for AI agents (skills, helper scripts, instructions, settings, hooks, MCP servers, commands) must work the same in both, so write it agent-neutrally:

- Say "the agent", not "Claude" or "Codex". Don't depend on one agent's tool names or parameters (Claude's `Agent`/`Task`, Codex's `spawn_agent`/`fork_context`); describe the intent instead ("start a fresh subagent with no prior context").
- Put shared pieces in shared places (`~/.ai/` for instructions and skills, `~/bin` for scripts, both tracked in the dotfiles repo), not under `~/.claude` or `~/.codex`.
- Anything that reads agent data (sessions, config, memory) handles both `~/.claude` and `~/.codex`.
- If one agent needs an extra file or setting (e.g. Codex's `agents/openai.yaml` for a skill), add the counterpart for the other agent, or note why there is none.
- If something can't be symmetric because only one agent has the feature, say so in the file and tell the user.

### LaTeX Writing

- In new LaTeX documents, handle abbreviations with the `acronym` package: `\acrodef{X}{full form}` in the preamble, `\ac{X}` in the text, and `\acresetall` after the abstract. Never define one by hand, and write out any abbreviation used only once. Proper names stay plain.
- In an existing document, follow its convention and ask before converting.
- Use `\acf{X}` in captions that may float above X's first use. Check the page limit after expanding.

